## Historical queue: receipt time versus merge time

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports continued after the bulk merge, through the October 4 snapshot. A queue update alone
was not evidence of resolution. The later October 5 engineering update, not the merge spike,
is the source for closing our issues. Use receipt timestamps to assess impact and merge timestamps to assess triage.

## Triage correction

Matching subjects can hide different bugs, and different subjects can describe the same bug.
Check tenant, form version, channel and cause before merging.
The executable cases below contradict the old subject-only rule.

<DedupeExplorer />

See the [current triage rule](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause).
The accepted 2024 decision remains unchanged as history, not current guidance.

## Actions and boundaries

- Track parser and hint as separate GitHub issues, with the computed membership lists above.
- Parser: the desk reports a fix; independent ambiguous-date regression testing was not supplied.
- Hint: the form was retired, so close as not planned rather than claiming a repaired hint.
- Preserve the unexplained reports and investigate their raw data; do not silently discard them.
- Reconcile with the desk before treating this export as current; publish subsequent deltas and review dates.
- Do not estimate affected people from report count: a report is not a unique person.
- Existing scope says sched-prod calendar invitations serve about 300k people, but this desk handles other products too.
  The export does not prove that every report, tenant or person belongs to sched-prod.

